I'm sorry, I don't know, either. I'll ask a station staff for you.
Is this the right platform for the trains to Kyoto?
Yes, it is. But what time does your train leave?
Twelve fourteen.
What is the number of your car?
I see. It's car eight. Your car will stop at the pillar number six.
No. This platform is for train going the opposite direction.
Your train leaves Platform ten over there.
Yes, it does. But the next express will get there sooner.
Excuse me. May I pass, please?
Excuse me. I'll put my things away right now.
You should put that baggage up on the rack.
Excuse me. I'm afraid this is my seat.
I'm sorry. I took the wrong seat.
Really? Will you let me see your ticket, please?
Your seat is in Car eleven.
This is car twelve.
Yes, you can see it clearly, because it's fine today.
A conductor is coming to check our tickets.
May I see your ticket, please?
Excuse me. Do you have the timetable for trains to Nara?
Yes. Do you need a connection?
This train will get to Kyoto at fifteen ten. So, you can take a train leaving Kyoto at fifteen twenty.
But I suggest you take the next rapid train which leaves Kyoto at fifteen forty. The arrival time at Nara will not be much different.
Excuse me. I think I've underpaid.
All right. Let me see your ticket, please.
The extra charge is two hundred and sixty yen, please.
Excuse me. Do you have change?
Yes, I have just the change for it.
Thank you very much. Here is your adjustment ticket.
There is only a driver on a bus, no conductor.
Some lines are taken at a flat fare, and others at different fares according to distance.
Drop the exact amount into the fare box.
No change will be returned in most cases.
When you don't have enough change, you should get your money changed at the coin machine.
Some machines return only the change after keeping the fare.
On buses with flat fare, you pay when you get on. On buses with distance fares you pay as you get off. This is a general rule.
When you pay after, you pick up a ticket as you get on.
The ticket has a number, and the fare varies according to the number.
In this case, a fare table is found near the driver's seat.
If you can't find the fare, show your ticket to the driver.
When you want to get off at the next stop, you just press the buzzer button.
Excuse me. Where is the nearest bus stop around here?
It's about five minutes on foot.
It's in front of the post office.
Pick up a ticket from the machine. You pay when you get off.
Many long-distance buses on major routes are night buses.
Buses are much cheaper than trains, so students often use them.
Buses are comfortable.
Seats are big and they recline.
The bus has a toilet and makes rest stops.
The bus runs at night when there is less traffic. So it is seldom delayed.
Taxis in Japan, as in other countries, run on meters.
The basic fare covers the first one point five to two kilometers. Beyond that the fare goes up according to distance or time.
The basic fare varies depending on the size of the car.
Both basic and additional fares may slightly vary from prefecture to prefecture.
Between eleven at night and five in the morning, special high rates are applied.
Taxis in Japan may be a little more expensive than in other countries.
But no tipping is necessary.
If you take a cab for a long distance, you don't have to pay for the return trip.
You don't have to pay extra for baggage, either.
The address numbers of Japanese houses are not much orderly.
And most streets do not have names or signs. So it is pretty difficult to get to a destination just by its address.
You cannot only get a cab at a depot, but you can flag one on the street.
You can also call a cab by telephone. In this case you have to pay a little extra.
Grand Hotel, please.
