It's too hard to explain by words. I'll draw a map for you.
Sorry, I don't know myself. I'll ask somebody for you.
This town is on the other side of the station. You took the opposite direction.
You should first go back to the station.
I've never heard of this address around here.
When you get out of the subway station, you'll see a large ad tower. That is the landmark.
There is only one exit.
Turn right at street.
At the West Exit of Shinjuku Station, take bus numbered seventy-four.
Turn left on the first road after the highway.
Do you know how to use a telephone?
A telephone card is handy.
You can use a telephone card on a green or gray telephone.
You can buy a telephone card at a train station kiosk or from a vending machine.
If you use a hundred yen coin, you won't get your change back.
For a local call, it's ten yen for one minute.
From here, you have to dial zero three first. Otherwise, you won't get through.
Sorry, you have the wrong number.
Isn't this three two five one one eight three six?
When do you expect her back?
She should be back in the evening.
I'll call her back later.
Would you please ask her to call me back?
I'll tell her to call you as soon as she returns.
Is there anybody who can speak English?
Would you speak a little more slowly?
Excuse me, but, who is calling, please?
Can you spell your name, please?
Hold on. I'll get the one in charge for you.
He is on another line now.
I'll ask him to call you back.
I want to send this parcel to the States.
What are you sending?
Do you want to send it airmail or surface mail?
I recommend that you buy insurance. It would cost you four hundred yen.
We can see flowers at any time of the year around Tokyo.
You have them at florists, don't you?
No, I mean flowers which grow.
Even in winter?
Plum trees bloom already in February, the coldest time of the year.
March is a time for peach blossoms.
You admire cherry blossoms in April of course.
Under cherry trees cherry-viewing parties are held.
Of autumn flowers in Japan, bush clovers and chrysanthemums are popular.
Ginkgo trees are very beautiful when their leaves turn yellow in the fall.
The autumn view of Japan, when maples and other trees change their colors, is as lovely as a picture.
The art of raising miniature trees in small ceramic pots. One does it for hobby.
They are miniature trees, but they aren't young, are they?
No, they aren't. Some are several decades old.
Why don't they grow tall?
Their roots are kept small.
Are the branches cut, too?
Yes. They cut back the branches and pick the buds to make nice-looking trees.
They are small, yet they have character.
That's right. They look like full-grown natural trees. People enjoy the scenery they produce.
We often see pine and plum trees among many others.
Does that include a hat and shoes?
No. Something to put on the body.
Many reasons for it.
Kimono is not very suitable for active people.
Also they're expensive.
Yes. I was surprised to see their prices at a department store.
A traditional bridal costume costs more than a million yen.
On New Year's days. And on special occasions such as wedding receptions, award ceremonies or funerals.
Young girls are likely to wear a kimono on Adult's Day, January fifteenth, and for their college graduation ceremony.
I don't, because I have no formal kimono.
Originally it was worn as a bathrobe. It's good to wear yukata for relaxing at home.
Yukata and happi are popular items for souvenirs of Japan for foreigners.
I bought a furin yesterday. What do you use it for?
We feel a touch of coolness with its sound.
Because summer in Japan is usually very hot, and we had no air-conditioning in the old days.
Gold fish is meant for the same purpose. We see it and feel cool.
Nothing special. They admire the flowers every morning.
I often see dogs and cats these days. Do the Japanese like animals?
Yes, there have always been many people who keep a dog or cat, or both.
There are a variety of dogs, aren't there?
Besides local breeds, many foreign dogs have been imported.
Some like the Kishu Dog are big, but most are medium sized.
What do you feed them? Dog food?
No. Leftovers in my case.
Where can you buy a puppy?
At a pet shop. They sell also kittens, squirrels and many others.
Cats are also popular as pets, aren't they?
Cats and dogs are rivals as pets. Those who like cats don't like dogs so much, and vice versa.
Which do you prefer, a dog or a cat?
Well, I like both.
How about birds and tropical fish?
Both are popular among children and women.
I hear that some keep a snake as pet. It's beyond my understanding.
I never liked reptiles, either.
How many national holidays in Japan?
We have fifteen national holidays.
You have many.
We have no national holidays associated with religions.
We don't have movable holidays either.
The fourth of May was named as a national holiday quite recently.
What is the significance?
It happens that May third and fifth are national holidays. So we decided to make three consecutive holidays by adding the fourth.
It is a policy to cope with the world opinion that the Japanese work too much.
It is said that a nation's history, tradition and culture is implied in its national holidays.
What holidays do you have in Japan?
Well, to begin with, January first is New Year's Day. It is called oshogatsu.
We celebrate having a new year after the old year passed safely.
Houses are decorated with kadomatsu and okazari, though it has been getting outmoded.
Women get dressed up.
New Year's dishes called osechi ryori are prepared at almost every home even now.
Millions of people visit famous shrines for the year's first worship.
January fifteenth is Adult's Day.
Young people who turned twenty during the past year are honored.
This was the day when the first Emperor ascended the throne according to our oldest history book.
Our present Emperor is the one hundred twenty-fifth Emperor.
March twentieth is the Vernal Equinox.
Depending on the year, it may be March twenty-first.
This is the middle day of the equinoctial week, and many families visit their ancestors' tombs.
April twenty-ninth was the birthday of Emperor Showa, father of the present Emperor. Now, it is celebrated as Green Day.
May third is Constitution Memorial Day.
On May third, nineteen forty-seven, our current national Constitution took effect.
May fifth is Children's Day.
It used to be a festive day to wish for the healthy growth of boys. The day was called Tango-no-sekku.
Today, the day's programs are for both boys and girls.
Carp streamers are hoisted and samurai dolls are displayed around this day.
There is the festive day for girls on March third.
September fifteenth is Respect-for-the-Aged Day.
Many programs for senior citizens are held.
September twenty-first is the Autumnal Equinox.
October tenth is Sports Day.
This holiday was established to commemorate the Tokyo Olympic Games in nineteen sixty-four.
November third is Culture Day.
Cultural festivals and events are held here and there.
Orders of Cultural Merits are given to distinguished artists and scholars on this day.
November twenty-third is Labor Thanksgiving Day.
In years past, it was a day to celebrate the rice harvest.
December twenty-third is the Emperor's Birthday.
We have many annual events besides national holidays.
February third is Setsubun, the day before spring begins by lunar calendar. It sometimes falls on February fourth.
We scatter roasted soy beans to drive off evil spirits.
It's a festival for girls, and many dolls in old court costumes are displayed.
The main event in April is cherry blossom viewing. A lot of folks have parties under cherry trees.
On July seventh is held Tanabata, Star Festival.
People decorate branches of bamboo with strips of paper and write their wishes on them.
Obon is a Buddhist festival to repose the spirits of one's ancestors. It's held in August in other areas.
It is believed that the spirits of one's ancestors return to their families during Obon.
Many people return to their home towns from cities to celebrate Obon. It's like a summer vacation.
In this season, bon-odori, or Bon dance, is performed throughout Japan.
Some people get together to admire the full moon at a night in September, more precisely on August fifteenth by the lunar calendar.
November fifteenth is Shichi-Go-San day, or the Seven-Five-Three Festival.
Seven-year-old girls, five-year-old boys and three-year-old boys and girls are taken to shrines.
Parents and children thank God that the children have been able to reach those ages safely.
The children are all dressed like adults.
December is again a gift season, which we call oseibo. We send gifts to those who were helpful to us.
We write New Year's cards and post them. They are delivered on January first.
On New Year's Eve, we watch and listen to temple bells ringing out the old year on TV while eating noodles. In this way we finish the year.
You should report it to the police immediately.
I left my bag in the cab.
Let's contact the taxi company.
I'll try to look for it.
What type of bag is it?
It's a black bag.
My name tag is on the bag.
What is in the bag?
They said they will contact you if they find it.
Write where we can contact you, please.
Is there anyone who speaks English?
Can I have a paper which certifies I was robbed?
There was a traffic accident.
Let's call the police.
Let's report to the rent-a car company.
What's the license number?
You should go to a hospital.
Do you remember how it happened?
You'll be given first aid.
What is your blood type?
Are you in pain?
You don't look well.
I have abdominal pain.
Where, specifically?
What kind of pain?
Is it a dull pain?
Let me take your temperature.
You should see a doctor.
Can I have medicine?
I'll write a prescription for you.
Please take this prescription to a pharmacy and buy the medicine.
Take this pill three times a day after meals.
The northern tip of Japan is as far north as Montreal, Canada.
The southernmost island of Japan extends as far down as the tip of Florida Peninsula.
Japan is mostly composed of four main islands.
In addition, there are more than three thousand smaller islands.
Japan faces the Pacific Ocean in the south and the Japan Sea in the north.
More than seventy percent of the land is forested mountains.
Because of it little land remains for cultivation.
The population of Japan is about one hundred twenty million.
Our population is on increasing, though very slightly.
The birth rate is decreasing.
The mortality rate is also decreasing.
Japanese life expectancy is the longest in the world.
Women live longer than men by about five years.
The population of aged people is steadily increasing.
One out of every four Japanese is above sixty-five years old.
Japan is a long country extending from north to south.
In Hokkaido, you can't play golf for about six months due to its cold climate.
On the contrary, Okinawa has a semitropical climate and you can swim in the sea the year round.
On Honshu island, the winters on the Japan Sea side and north of Tokyo are very cold.
Anywhere in Japan, except the extreme south and north, we have four distinct seasons.
We have also a rainy season in Japan, which lasts from early June to mid-July.
The rainy season is called tsuyu. We write it as the rain of plum, because it comes around the time the plums ripen.
It's not only hot but very muggy in summer.
Toward the end of the summer unwelcome guests visit us every year.
They are typhoons. Once hitting Japan they cause us a lot of disasters.
In autumn, leaves of many trees turn red or yellow, and they are very beautiful.
Autumn is the best season for getting around Japan with its comfortable climate.
In winter the air is dry.
We have much sunshine on the Pacific Ocean side, but many cloudy days along the Sea of Japan.
Japan is an old country, isn't it?
Yes. But, not as old as China.
How old is Japan?
The Jomon period was from about ten thousand B.C. to about three hundred B.C.
The Yayoi period was from around three hundred B.C. to about three hundred A.D.
We have no written records earlier than the fourth century.
The Chinese characters were brought in during the fourth century.
There are many things from those days in Nara.
Horyuji Temple which is the oldest wooden building in the world is worth seeing.
Prince Shotoku built it. He protected and propagated Buddhism.
After his death, there was a political reform, which is known as the Reform of Taika Era.
Then, the first constitutional form of government was established.
In seven ten, Nara became the capital and Buddhism flourished in those days.
At the end of the eighth century, the capital was moved to Kyoto.
After that the nobility started to control the land and its people.
Later, the land began to be owned privately and it created the warrior class, which grew stronger in generations.
Buddhism prospered during the age of samurai. Zen Buddhism, above all, was supported by the warrior class.
From the fourteenth through the sixteenth centuries, Japan was politically unstable.
Feudal lords throughout the country vied for power, and battles were fought almost daily.
Guns introduced to Japan at that time played an important role in winning battles.
During that period Europeans came to Japan for the first time.
At the same time Christianity was brought to Japan by Jesuit missionaries.
Christianity was banned and the country was closed to the rest of the world.
The Edo era, with no wars in the country, lasted for about two hundred fifty years.
Nagasaki was the only window open for trade with the Dutch, Chinese and Koreans.
Commerce and handicraft made progress during the Edo period.
Culture flourished, with activities such as Kabuki plays, Ukiyoe paintings, literature and ceramic art becoming popular.
After the American fleet of "Black Ships" came in the mid-nineteenth century, Japan opened its doors to the world.
Japan made its way to modernization rapidly.
The government supported the promotion of industries.
Education was emphasized and military power increased.
In World War II, Japan fought America and Britain.
Democracy has become the basis of all.
The legislation, the administration and the judiciary are independent of each other.
The Diet consists of the House of Representatives and the House of Councilors.
All Diet members are directly elected by the people.
There is no presidency.
The Prime Minister is appointed by the Diet.
At present, the head of the majority party is the Prime Minister.
The Prime Minister organizes his cabinet by appointing ministers.
Members of the House of Representatives take office for four years.
However, the Prime Minister can dissolve the House of Representatives, representing the cabinet.
If the House is dissolved, general election will be held.
Members of the House of Councilors remain in office for six years, while half of the members undergo election every three years.
What is the minimum voting age in Japan?
How many central government offices are there?
There are about twenty.
MITI is well known even outside Japan.
It's the Ministry of International Trade and Industry. It promoted foreign trade after the war, that's why.
Within the country, the budget of the Ministry of Health and Welfare has become large.
Among local governments, the Tokyo Metropolitan government is by far the biggest.
There are forty-six. Each prefecture is headed by a governor, and each has its own assembly.
How many political parties are there?
I don't know the exact number. The Liberal-Democratic Party dominates the government.
I understand that Japan does not have a military.
We have self-defense forces.
But we cannot use them for anything other than self-defense.
Japan's postwar economy has made remarkable progress, hasn't it?
Yes. Now Japan has the second largest GNP in the world.
It's the total value of goods and services produced minus the cost of raw materials.
It's used as a measure of a nation's economic activity.
What is the rate of economic growth?
Our growth has recently slowed down, and the rate is now about three to four percent.
From nineteen fifty-five into the sixties the growth rate was over ten percent a year.
What made this high growth possible?
I think there are many reasons.
We must import raw materials and resources of energy.
But we had good quality labor.
We brought in new technology and equipment from other countries.
With products processed in Japan, we expanded our export market.
Japan exports more than it imports.
The trade balance is in the black, isn't it?
The surplus is getting smaller.
The surplus is getting bigger again.
Japan's domestic demand is decreasing.
Manufacturers try to increase exports.
Business isn't getting better in spite of lower interest rates.
Interest rates will be further lowered.
Automobile manufacturing is Japan's key industry.
Our automobile companies are competing in the international market.
Are they all exported cars?
No. They are manufactured in various countries.
There aren't many American cars in Japan.
American companies don't make their cars in Japan.
American cars used to be a symbol of wealth.
American computer companies are doing well in Japan.
What other companies do well?
Is it difficult to find a job?
It's more difficult than last year.
There aren't many jobs.
You can't change your job easily.
The Japanese don't change their jobs so often.
But I hear that women often change their jobs.
Are there many women who become executives?
No, very few. That may be one of the reasons why women don't stick with one company.
How is the stock market these days?
Stock markets in the States seem to be good. The markets in Japan are at their worst.
Because the performance of most major companies is getting worse.
When do you think it will get better?
It will be several months before the recovery.
You should not sell stock now, if you have any.
Yes, it's a problem of waste disposal.
You have few places to dump waste.
So, we are trying to reuse some of the waste.
The amount of industrial waste is reduced by treatment.
Then, the problem of environmental contamination is also reduced. Right?
Yes, the treatment aims at environmental friendliness.
But the cost increases, doesn't it?
Can Japan produce all the food that in needs?
Never. Only about half.
But you are self-sufficient in your staple, rice, correct?
Our rice consumption is decreasing.
Is that so? How come?
Young people eat more bread than ever.
Those who eat noodles for lunch are also increasing.
Instant noodles also sell well.
Its price is lower.
That helps us.
We also buy lots of seafood from other countries.
But you grow vegetables here.
Imports of vegetables are also increasing.
I received the invitation card from Haruko-san for her wedding reception.
It's June fifteenth. I'm told that it is a lucky day called taian.
I see. She'll be a June bride.
Usually men wear a black formal suit. A dinner jacket will be all right of course.
And you wear a black bow tie or a white necktie.
What do you usually give as a wedding present in Japan?
We don't bring any presents to the reception. Instead, the guests bring cash.
You put the money in a special decorated envelope which is called shugibukuro.
How much should I give?
It is always a question. It will be all right if you ask some friends and bring a similar amount.
I was also asked to make a speech.
That's wonderful. Will you speak in English or Japanese?
I'm thinking of speaking in English, because one of Haruko-san's friends agreed to interpret.
How do you dress when attending a funeral?
We wear black suit with black tie.
Some people carry with them a rosary.
Does a funeral take place at a temple?
If the person was a Buddhist, yes. But many families hold funerals at their homes.
Each of the mourners offers incense before the deceased.
In the case of a funeral, too, it is customary for the mourners to bring cash in an envelope, which is called okoden.
It is meant to aid the bereaved with the funeral expenses.
Half the amount of okoden is returned in the form of goods as a token of thanks.
Young Japanese are sensitive to fashion.
They eagerly learn the latest fashion from TV and magazines.
But they behave differently.
How differently?
For example, I've never seen a young couple kissing in the public.
I see. You don't mind kissing in public in America. I've seen such scenes in movies.
It's a greeting there.
Conversely, I sometimes see something I don't see in the States.
Pairs of women are seen walking on the street hand in hand.
Yes, we see them, young women. They are good friends.
In the States, we may think the couple have a relationship more than just friends.
Speaking of touching, an American man puts his arm around the back of a lady in a natural manner.
Do ladies accept that?
Sure, as long as it's in a natural way.
It's a matter of the custom. In Japan, there used to be a moral code that a boy mustn't even sit with a girl after they get to be seven years old.
Was it followed?
It's probably a well-known kindergarten.
Why is it so well-known?
That's because many graduates from the kindergarten enter reputable elementary schools.
What are reputable elementary schools?
Well, it's the elementary school which sends many graduates to good junior high schools.
I see. Then, there must be the universities that everyone wishes to enter.
Why does this happen?
The graduates from good universities can benefit from it in society.
What preparations do they make to succeed in the exam?
Usually they go to preparatory or cram schools, or get tutors.
That's expensive, isn't it?
Do you have no other way to enter a university than pass the exam?
Yes, we have. Recently, the number of the colleges which admit entrance of students recommended by the graduating schools is increasing.
I'm told that the exam fee and tuition are costly.
Yes, they are very expensive in case of the private colleges.
How many exams will a student take?
One student I know sent more than ten applications.
When I went to Kyoto the other day, I saw many groups of students.
Is that a study trip?
Yes. They study before the trip and write a report afterward.
Some even go to foreign countries for school excursions now.
Luxurious. Parents pay for it of course?
Yes, they do. Money for many things.
